1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,025
If an automatic door can open the door,

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:03,250
why pay a doorman?

3
00:00:03,675 --> 00:00:05,625
That's a trap called the doorman fallacy.

4
00:00:06,038 --> 00:00:09,025
MIT mathematician Ankur Moitra brought it up on

5
00:00:09,038 --> 00:00:10,500
Lex Fridman's podcast,

6
00:00:10,888 --> 00:00:14,000
right after AI solved problems mathematicians had chased

7
00:00:14,012 --> 00:00:14,750
for decades.

8
00:00:15,162 --> 00:00:17,138
A doorman doesn't just open doors.

9
00:00:17,562 --> 00:00:18,250
He welcomes you,

10
00:00:18,738 --> 00:00:19,550
recognizes you,

11
00:00:19,962 --> 00:00:20,838
keeps you safe,

12
00:00:21,138 --> 00:00:22,425
and makes you feel cared for.

13
00:00:22,838 --> 00:00:24,325
Replace him with a sliding door,

14
00:00:24,625 --> 00:00:25,712
and you lose all of that.

15
00:00:26,125 --> 00:00:28,262
Your job is not its most visible task

16
00:00:28,275 --> 00:00:28,550
either.

17
00:00:28,962 --> 00:00:30,800
And when problem solving becomes cheap,

18
00:00:31,225 --> 00:00:33,562
everything else you do becomes more valuable.

19
00:00:34,062 --> 00:00:35,850
Full animated breakdown on my channel,

20
00:00:36,138 --> 00:00:36,825
so subscribe.
